","inflection of reuig:",0],[410071,"reuigeren","adj","strong genitive masculine/neuter singular comparative degree",1],[410072,"reuigeren","adj","weak/mixed genitive/dative all-gender singular comparative degree",2],[410073,"reuigeren","adj","strong/weak/mixed accusative masculine singular comparative degree",3],[410074,"reuigeren","adj","strong dative plural comparative degree",4],[410075,"reuigeren","adj","weak/mixed all-case plural comparative degree",5]],"inflections":[["reuigeren","reuig","comparative,genitive,masculine,singular,strong,without-article"]]},"rostbraten":{"entries":[["rostbraten",null,"/ˈʁɔstˌbʁaːtn̩/","Rost (“grill”) + Braten (“roast”)",{"g":"m","decl":"|||s||||"}]],"senses":[[604592,"rostbraten","noun","a roast cooked on a grill",0,"m"],[604593,"rostbraten","noun","prime rib",1,"m",["Austria"]]]},"schnee":{"entries":[["schnee",null,"/ʃneː/","From Middle High German snê, from Old High German snēo, from Proto-West Germanic *snaiw.",{"g":"m","decl":"|||s|-|-|-|-"}]],"senses":[[2700,"schnee","noun","snow",0,"m"],[2701,"schnee","noun","snow (static on TV set etc.)",1,"m",["figuratively"]],[2702,"schnee","noun","cocaine",2,"m",["slang"]]],"related_forms":[[3819,"schnee","schneeig","derived"],[3820,"schnee","schnee von gestern","related"],[3821,"schnee","schneeball","related"],[3822,"schnee","schneebedeckt","related"],[3823,"schnee","schneeberg","related"],[3824,"schnee","schneebesen","related"],[3825,"schnee","schneeblind","related"],[3826,"schnee","schneeblindheit","related"],[3827,"schnee","schneefall","related"],[3828,"schnee","schneeflocke","related"],[3829,"schnee","schneefrei","related"],[3830,"schnee","schneegestöber","related"],[3831,"schnee","schneeglöckchen","related"],[3832,"schnee","schneekanone","related"],[3833,"schnee","schneemann","related"],[3834,"schnee","schneepflug","related"],[3835,"schnee","schneeregen","related"],[3836,"schnee","schneereich","related"],[3837,"schnee","schneereifen","related"],[3838,"schnee","schneeschaf","related"],[3839,"schnee","schneeschauer","related"],[3840,"schnee","schneeschmelze","related"],[3841,"schnee","schneesicher","related"],[3842,"schnee","schneesturm","related"],[3843,"schnee","schneeverwehung","related"],[3844,"schnee","schneeweiß","related"],[3845,"schnee","schneewittchen","related"],[3846,"schnee","schneeziege","related"],[3847,"schnee","schneien","related"]]},"schummeln":{"entries":[["schummeln",null,"/ˈʃʊməln/","There are two main theories, which need not be mutually exclusive but may complement each other.\n* From dialectal schummeln, originally probably “to move back and forth”, whence various attested senses: “waver, totter; rock, swing, push; chase off; abscond, steal away; take clandestinely; rummage; bustle about; do housework”, etc. In favour of this derivation speaks that the sense “to cheat” was sometimes associated with “trickery through swift movements”, especially in the context of gambling. The verb schummeln (in the above-mentioned senses) is found in dialects as far south as Tyrol, but Adelung considered it chiefly Low German and the earliest attestations are of late Middle Dutch scommelen (1410: “to do kitchenwork”, 1530: “to rock, move back and forth”); hence probably of northern origin through Middle Low German *schummelen, eventually perhaps onomatopoeic. Compare modern Dutch schommelen. Compare also German verschaukeln (“to cheat”), from schaukeln (“to rock, swing”).\n* Through a dialectally attested sense “to trade (of a Jewish merchant)”, from Yiddish שום (shum), an acronym for the cities of Speyer, Worms, Mainz, the so-called Schum cities, old centres of Jewish religion and trade. However, a verb derived from this acronym is not attested in Yiddish and the sense “to trade” may be derived from the sense “to cheat”, rather than vice versa.",{"g":"n","decl":"|||s|-|-|-|-"}]],"senses":[[240559,"schummeln","verb","to cheat (especially in games, but also in business, school, etc.)",0,null,["informal"]],[240560,"schummeln","noun","geru